        "question": "State and explain Euclid's proof for the infinity of prime numbers.",
        "answerKey": "1. **Euclid's Proof**: - Suppose there are only finitely many primes, say $p_1, p_2, \\dots, p_n$. - Consider the number $P = (p_1 p_2 \\dots p_n) + 1$. - Since $P > 1$, by the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic, $P$ must be divisible by at least one prime $q$. - If $q$ is one of our listed primes $p_i$, then $q$ divides the product $p_1 \\dots p_n$. Since $q$ also divides $P$, it must divide the difference $P - (p_1 \\dots p_n) = 1$, which is impossible. - Thus, $q$ is a new prime not in our list, contradicting the assumption of finiteness. Hence, primes are infinite. 2. **Euler's Proof**: - Euler proved that the sum of the reciprocals of all primes diverges: $$\\sum_{p \\text{ prime}} \\frac{1}{p} = \\infty$$ - This divergence implies there must be infinitely many primes, as a finite sum would always converge."
