        "question": "State the fundamental theorem of homomorphism. Define $\\varphi:(\\mathbb{R},+)\\to(\\{z\\in\\mathbb{C}:|z|=1\\},\\cdot)$ by $\\varphi(x)=e^{2\\pi ix}$. Show $\\varphi$ is a homomorphism, find $\\ker\\varphi$, and identify the quotient group.",
        "answerKey": "1. **Statement**: Let $\\phi: G \\to G'$ be a group homomorphism with kernel $K = \\text{Ker } \\phi$. Then the quotient group $G/K$ is isomorphic to the image $\\text{Im } \\phi$. That is: $$G/K \\cong \\phi(G)$$ 2. **Proof**: - Define a map $f: G/K \\to \\phi(G)$ by $f(gK) = \\phi(g)$ for all $g \\in G$. - **Well-defined**: If $aK = bK$, then $a^{-1}b \\in K = \\text{Ker } \\phi \\implies \\phi(a^{-1}b) = e' \\implies \\phi(a)^{-1}\\phi(b) = e' \\implies \\phi(a) = \\phi(b) \\implies f(aK) = f(bK)$. - **Homomorphism**: $f((aK)(bK)) = f(abK) = \\phi(ab) = \\phi(a)\\phi(b) = f(aK)f(bK)$. - **One-one**: If $f(aK) = f(bK)$, then $\\phi(a) = \\phi(b) \\implies \\phi(a)^{-1}\\phi(b) = e' \\implies \\phi(a^{-1}b) = e' \\implies a^{-1}b \\in K \\implies aK = bK$. - **Onto**: For any $y \\in \\phi(G)$, there exists $g \\in G$ such that $\\phi(g) = y$. Thus, $f(gK) = y$. - Therefore, $f$ is a well-defined group isomorphism, which implies $G/K \\cong \\phi(G)$."
