        "answerKey": "1. **Statement**: For any vectors $x$ and $y$ in an inner product space $V(F)$: $$|\\langle x, y \\rangle| \\le \\|x\\| \\|y\\|$$ 2. **Proof**: - If $y = 0$, then $|\\langle x, 0 \\rangle| = 0$ and $\\|x\\| \\|0\\| = 0$, so the inequality holds with equality. - If $y \\neq 0$, then for any scalar $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$, by positivity of the inner product: $$0 \\le \\langle x - t y, x - t y \\rangle = \\langle x, x \\rangle - 2t \\text{Re}\\langle x, y \\rangle + t^2 \\langle y, y \\rangle$$ - Choose $t = \\frac{\\langle x, y \\rangle}{\\langle y, y \\rangle}$ (assuming real inner product space for simplicity): $$0 \\le \\|x\\|^2 - 2\\frac{\\langle x, y \\rangle^2}{\\|y\\|^2} + \\frac{\\langle x, y \\rangle^2}{\\|y\\|^4} \\|y\\|^2 = \\|x\\|^2 - \\frac{\\langle x, y \\rangle^2}{\\|y\\|^2}$$ - Multiplying by $\\d\\|y\\|^2$: $$\\langle x, y \\rangle^2 \\le \\|x\\|^2 \\|y\\|^2 \\implies |\\langle x, y \\rangle| \\le \\|x\\| \\|y\\|$$ - This completes the proof."
