        "question": "State and prove the Bolzano-Weierstrass Theorem for sets.",
        "answerKey": "1. **Statement**: Every bounded sequence of real numbers has a convergent subsequence. 2. **Proof Sketch**: - Let $\\{x_n\\}$ be a bounded sequence. Thus, there exists an interval $I_0 = [a, b]$ containing all terms of the sequence. - Bisect $I_0$ into two subintervals $I_1' = [a, (a+b)/2]$ and $I_1'' = [(a+b)/2, b]$. At least one of these subintervals must contain infinitely many terms of $\\{x_n\\}$. Choose this interval and denote it as $I_1 = [a_1, b_1]$. - Repeat this bisection process. We get a nested sequence of intervals $I_k = [a_k, b_k]$ such that the length of $I_k$ is $(b-a)/2^k$ and each $I_k$ contains infinitely many terms of the sequence. - By the Nested Intervals Theorem, there exists a unique point $\\xi \\in \\bigcap_{k=1}^{\\infty} I_k$. - Construct a subsequence $\\{x_{n_k}\\}$ by choosing $x_{n_k} \\in I_k$ with $n_k > n_{k-1}$. - Since $a_k \\le x_{n_k} \\le b_k$ and $\\lim a_k = \\lim b_k = \\xi$, by the Squeeze Theorem, $\\lim_{k\\to\\infty} x_{n_k} = \\xi$. - Thus, we have found a convergent subsequence."
