        "answerKey": "1. **Iron Extraction Overview**: - Iron is extracted from its hematite ore ($\\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3$) in a blast furnace. The raw materials added are ore, coke (reducing agent and fuel), and limestone (flux). 2. **Blast Furnace Zones and Reactions**: - **Combustion Zone (Bottom)**: Coke burns to carbon dioxide: $$\\text{C} + \\text{O}_2 \\to \\text{CO}_2 \\quad (\\text{highly exothermic, } T \\approx 2000\\text{ K})$$ - **Reduction Zone (Top)**: Carbon monoxide reduces iron oxide in steps: $$3\\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3 + \\text{CO} \\to 2\\text{Fe}_3\\text{O}_4 + \\text{CO}_2$$ $$\\text{Fe}_3\\text{O}_4 + \\text{CO} \\to 3\\text{FeO} + \\text{CO}_2$$ $$\\text{FeO} + \\text{CO} \\to \\text{Fe(l)} + \\text{CO}_2 \\quad (T \\approx 800 - 1000\\text{ K})$$ - **Slag Formation**: Limestone decomposes to calcium oxide, which reacts with silica impurity to form fusible slag: $$\\text{CaCO}_3 \\to \\text{CaO} + \\text{CO}_2$$ $$\\text{CaO} + \\text{SiO}_2 \\to \\text{CaSiO}_3\\text{(l)} \\quad (\\text{slag})$$ - Slag floats on top of the molten iron and prevents re-oxidation of iron."
